00:23:24,750 --> 00:23:32,190
confine scientific research. So, we take this note and transfer of material as well.

205
00:23:33,150 --> 00:23:40,110
Materials can be transferred within overlaps using the proper biosecurity or biosafety protocols.

206
00:23:40,110 --> 00:23:45,390
And what one needs to do is maintain the chain of custody, a written chain of custody with

207
00:23:45,390 --> 00:23:50,510
documentation, a clear definition of roles, responsibilities and remedial measures in the

208
00:23:50,510 --> 00:23:57,150
case of breach of containment and the key aspect is accountability. And this accountability comes

209
00:23:57,150 --> 00:24:03,790
in terms of both personal integrity as well as in terms of the documentation to track the

210
00:24:04,670 --> 00:24:08,510
process of manipulation of a biological agent.

211
00:24:13,390 --> 00:24:24,990
The next part of the WHO laboratory biosafety manual refers to laboratory equipment. One of

212
00:24:24,990 --> 00:24:30,270
the laboratory equipment which has been described in detail is the biological safety cabinet.

213
00:24:30,270 --> 00:24:36,510
Biological safety cabinets are designed to protect the operator and the laboratory environment

214
00:24:36,510 --> 00:24:41,710
and work material from exposure to infectious aerosols and splashes that may be generated

215
00:24:41,710 --> 00:24:46,510
when manipulating materials containing infectious agents such as primary cultures,

216
00:24:46,510 --> 00:24:54,990
stocks and diagnostic specimens. What a biological safety cabinet offers to you is a safe working

217
00:24:54,990 --> 00:25:00,910
space. However, in order to utilize a biological safety cabinet, one has to take into account

218
00:25:00,910 --> 00:25:06,270
certain precautions such as the administrative controls and standard operating procedures.

219
00:25:10,140 --> 00:25:13,420
This is a class 1 biological safety cabinet.

220
00:25:15,100 --> 00:25:20,620
And a class 1 biological safety cabinet is basically a workspace in which you will note

221
00:25:20,620 --> 00:25:27,420
the air enters from the outside space and it is exited or exhausted through a HEPA

222
00:25:27,420 --> 00:25:33,660
filter into the environment. Now, this duct may lead outside the laboratory into the

223
00:25:34,140 --> 00:25:39,660
environment or in some cases it may lead back into the workspace. However, it is recommended

224
00:25:39,660 --> 00:25:44,940
that it lead to the external environment. Now, air the operator will stand in front of this

225
00:25:44,940 --> 00:25:51,020
safety cabinet operating this instrument over here and he or she will work within the confines

226
00:25:51,020 --> 00:25:58,140
of this space. The air circulates within this space and a major component of the air is exited

227
00:25:58,140 --> 00:26:04,860
or exhausted into the external environment via a HEPA filter. This is the basic level

228
00:26:04,860 --> 00:26:14,960
of protection to the operator. The class 2 B1 exhausts 70 percent of the air and recirculates

229
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:23,120
30 percent of the air and the HEPA filter in all cases traps 99.97 percent of their particles

230
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:31,120
which is of 0.3 micron in diameter. Now, you will ask me what happens to

231
00:26:31,120 --> 00:26:36,560
particles which are smaller in size than 0.3 micron. What you should note that is that

232
00:26:36,560 --> 00:26:44,480
a HEPA filter consists of multiple layers of the filtering medium. So, any particle which

233
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:49,440
bypasses the first level will actually get trapped in the second level and so on and so forth.

234
00:26:49,440 --> 00:26:55,360
Secondly, the HEPA filter reduces the velocity of flow of air. So, when the velocity of flow

235
00:26:55,360 --> 00:26:59,920
of air is reduced, the particles tend to get trapped within the HEPA filter itself.

236
00:27:00,720 --> 00:27:05,680
So, this is one of the considerations which you need to take into account when you raise

237
00:27:05,680 --> 00:27:11,920
that question about the size of the virus of other pathogens and their likelihood of passing

238
00:27:11,920 --> 00:27:21,840
through the HEPA filter. This is how the class 2 B1 filter actually works. So, we have the

239
00:27:21,840 --> 00:27:27,520
biological agent here I have indicated as a DNA molecule and the A is basically drawn through

240
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:34,000
these filters HEPA filter and the A which enters onto the top of the bench is actually HEPA filter

241
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:39,280
and it exists through this small grill at the bottom as well as over the specimen for

242
00:27:39,280 --> 00:27:45,520
the biological agent itself and then it is exhausted through the facility via the HEPA filter.

243
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:53,920
Again, the technical term if it is linked to the external environment is a hard ducted cabinet.

244
00:27:53,920 --> 00:28:00,000
However, if it recycles the air through your facility itself, it is not considered hard ducted,

245
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
it is allowing the air to filter back into the facility. So, this depends on your engineering

246
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:11,840
design. You have to take into account the fact that exhausting large amounts of your

247
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:18,320
filtered air from your facility may result in an increased load on your air conditioning system.

248
00:28:18,320 --> 00:28:27,870
This must be discussed with your engineers. Now, when you work in a biological safety cabinet,

249
00:28:27,870 --> 00:28:35,550
I have set up my cabinet this way. So, my pathogen or biological agent is here.

250
00:28:35,550 --> 00:28:40,590
This is my area in which I work with the pathogen and this is my base bin. Now,

251
00:28:40,590 --> 00:28:49,630
I will work in this direction. So, I will retrieve the specimen here. I will pick up a pipette tip

252
00:28:49,630 --> 00:28:55,150
and I will transfer the specimen onto this particular well. Now, if you will note here,

253
00:28:55,150 --> 00:29:01,310
I have put in absorbent towel in order to absorb any spills. This is a C-fold towel

254
00:29:02,190 --> 00:29:06,990
and I am using double bagging which I have discussed in another one of my videos on double

255
00:29:06,990 --> 00:29:13,790
bagging to dispose of the waste. I am working in this direction and please note that this is the

256
00:29:13,790 --> 00:29:21,870
grill. One must not block this grill as this grill is permitting a curtain of air to flow around

257
00:29:21,870 --> 00:29:26,830
and protecting you from the specimen and the specimen from you. So, the biological agent is

258
00:29:26,830 --> 00:29:31,790
protected on both sides and this is critical in diagnostic labs and the air actually flows

259
00:29:31,790 --> 00:29:36,990
through this. So, please do not block these grills as well and there will be a downdraft of air from

260
00:29:36,990 --> 00:29:41,950
above. There is a filter on top which creates a downdraft of air onto your workspace. So,

261
00:29:41,950 --> 00:29:49,260
this ensures that you are safe. These are some points which you must note. First, your facility

262
00:29:49,260 --> 00:29:55,980
must be certified periodically by this specific service provider. So, there is a certification

263
00:29:55,980 --> 00:30:01,420
procedure. The facility should be located away from drafts of air. There should be no air

264
00:30:01,420 --> 00:30:07,980
circulation near the biological safety cabinet. The placement of your material I have discussed

265
00:30:07,980 --> 00:30:14,860
here in this particular photograph. The operation should be in accordance with standard operating

266
00:30:14,860 --> 00:30:22,220
procedures. For instance, in my facility I may have a procedure for the decontamination prior

267
00:30:22,220 --> 00:30:29,020
to use using ultraviolet light for duration of 20 minutes followed by a surface wipe down

268
00:30:29,020 --> 00:30:35,820
and a decontamination post usage and this is stated in the cleaning and sterilization standard

269
00:30:35,820 --> 00:30:40,780
operating procedures which accompany this BIC. Finally, there are specific protocols for

270
00:30:40,780 --> 00:30:50,060
disinfection in case of a large scale. This section can actually be removed and then you

271
00:30:50,060 --> 00:30:58,540
can clean the area below and disinfect it with a specific agent and with the specific contact time

272
00:30:58,540 --> 00:31:03,340
and finally there is maintenance schedule. So, you have breakdown and

273
00:31:03,340 --> 00:31:13,280
theoretical maintenance in your SOPs. We now move on to the next part which is good microbiological

274
00:31:13,280 --> 00:31:22,240
techniques. So, good microbiological techniques are an element of biosafety and these are

275
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:27,200
pertained to laboratory procedures which are listed in your standard operating procedures,

276
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:32,960
contingency plans and emergency procedures in case of a breach of containment or in the case of

277
00:31:32,960 --> 00:31:40,080
for example, you may have laboratory operators who may get sick in the middle of a procedure and

278
00:31:40,080 --> 00:31:44,720
they may have to be evacuated. There has to be an emergency procedure in place for this

279
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:51,200
as well as large spills and fires and disinfection of infectious and biological agents

280
00:31:52,160 --> 00:31:58,720
using specific agents with the requisite content over the contact time and finally

281
00:31:58,720 --> 00:32:04,800
the transport of biological agents from one facility to another or within the facility itself.

282
00:32:08,670 --> 00:32:15,470
The transport of infectious material is one of the cases in point in which you may require to

283
00:32:15,470 --> 00:32:21,710
transport your biological agent from your facility to another state national or regional

284
00:32:21,710 --> 00:32:29,310
or even maybe a global facility because you do not have the requisite resources to manage

285
00:32:29,310 --> 00:32:36,430
that biological agent in your setting. In that case one has to resort to transport protocols.

286
00:32:37,230 --> 00:32:42,990
So, transport protocols reduce the likelihood of that package being damaged or leaking,

287
00:32:42,990 --> 00:32:49,950
reduce exposures which can result in possible infection as your package may travel through the

288
00:32:49,950 --> 00:32:55,550
national network of transports including the airline system and they improve efficiency of

289
00:32:55,550 --> 00:33:03,620
package delivery. This is why proper labeling is very important. These are some of the references

290
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:10,340
International Air Transport Association, IATA infectious substances shipping guidelines

291
00:33:10,340 --> 00:33:16,500
and they are updated currently and you need to refer to the current update

292
00:33:16,500 --> 00:33:22,100
in order to obtain the latest guidance and that is what is known as a basic triple packaging system

293
00:33:22,100 --> 00:33:29,780
which I will discuss in the next slide and you must ship the consignment along with a spill

294
00:33:29,780 --> 00:33:36,500
cleanup procedure. However, this is highly unlikely as the material is triple packaged but you must

295
00:33:36,500 --> 00:33:46,020
still expect or anticipate this possibility of a spill cleanup in the lab or in the aeroplane or

296
00:33:46,020 --> 00:33:53,780
in the shipping system. This is a basic triple packaging. I have made a schematic diagram for you.

297
00:33:53,780 --> 00:33:58,660
Now, this is the biological agent itself. It is right at the center of this. This is surrounded

298
00:33:58,660 --> 00:34:03,780
by your first layer of protection which is waterproof. This can be in the form of a vial

299
00:34:03,780 --> 00:34:10,500
which is a falcon tube. Please do not use glass for this unless the biological agent requires

300
00:34:10,500 --> 00:34:17,700
transport in glass. Usually, we use the polypropylene tubes with airtight locks.

301
00:34:18,500 --> 00:34:23,300
This is in turn surrounded by what is known as an absorbent material. For example, if you are

302
00:34:23,300 --> 00:34:30,820
shipping 50 ml of biological agent in a liquid medium, please ensure that your absorbent

303
00:34:30,820 --> 00:34:36,420
material can absorb at least 3 times this amount, minimal. You can absorb, you can put

304
00:34:36,420 --> 00:34:41,140
in additional material and padding. However, please ensure that you can absorb. You need to

305
00:34:41,140 --> 00:34:46,020
validate this in the laboratory setting in a biological safety cabinet. And then you have

306
00:34:46,020 --> 00:34:52,900
your next layer which is basically your secondary waterproof container. So, in case of an impact,

307
00:34:52,900 --> 00:34:57,780
this container will crack. However, the internal container is protected. And finally, you have

308
00:34:57,780 --> 00:35:04,100
your tertiary packaging which may be in the form of your box, your cardboard box or your plastic

309
00:35:04,100 --> 00:35:10,180
box. And this should all be labeled. This is very important that you use the appropriate labels for

310
00:35:10,180 --> 00:35:16,180
this particular package. Label it clearly. If it's going to cross national boundaries,

311
00:35:16,180 --> 00:35:25,060
please label it in the language of that particular jurisdiction or else your labels in English or

312
00:35:25,060 --> 00:35:32,020
French or whichever language you have used at the origin may not be deciphered in the country

313
00:35:32,020 --> 00:35:42,210
of the recipient. So, this needs to be taken into account. We now move on to the next part

314
00:35:42,210 --> 00:35:47,730
which is the introduction to biotechnology. Now, the World Health Organization has recognized the

315
00:35:47,730 --> 00:35:55,970
challenge faced by labs in terms of genetic engineering and synthetic biology. So, for those

316
00:35:55,970 --> 00:36:03,330
of us who are aware, genetic engineering involves the introduction of genes into a specific organism

317
00:36:03,890 --> 00:36:11,490
which are beyond the range of transmission of that organism. So, in biological systems,

318
00:36:11,490 --> 00:36:16,210
we have genes which are transmitted vertically, which means they are transmitted from the parent

319
00:36:16,210 --> 00:36:22,290
to the progeny. And we also have genes which are transferred horizontally. This may be from

320
00:36:22,290 --> 00:36:27,650
another organism or via genetic engineering. Now, the genes may be introduced with

321
00:36:28,610 --> 00:36:37,250
an intent to enhance the organism's capabilities. However, there is something which one needs to be

322
00:36:37,250 --> 00:36:44,610
aware of, which is called the gain of function experiments. Many national laws do not permit

323
00:36:44,610 --> 00:36:50,450
gain of function experiments which increase the virulence of the biological agent over the

324
00:36:50,450 --> 00:36:56,050
force range. So, there are some traits which are increased ability to get transmitted.

325
00:36:56,850 --> 00:37:02,290
For instance, if you have a virus which has a specific type of receptor, a researcher may

326
00:37:02,290 --> 00:37:07,410
introduce a new type of receptor which increases its pathogenicity. And these gain of function

327
00:37:07,410 --> 00:37:14,290
experiments are good for researchers in terms of the ability to identify threats. However,

328
00:37:14,290 --> 00:37:19,010
if these biological agents are stolen or if there is a breach of containment,

329
00:37:19,010 --> 00:37:27,090
there will be consequences. So, this is one aspect which is the gain of function aspect.

330
00:37:27,090 --> 00:37:32,370
The other aspect is the dual use research of concern or DURC. This is also a particular

331
00:37:33,410 --> 00:37:41,090
area of concern to virus managers as a pathogen which is benign can be utilized for dual use.

332
00:37:42,050 --> 00:37:50,450
And this risk assessment must be done in consultation with your national security agencies.

333
00:37:54,110 --> 00:37:59,390
Okay, so this is a simple graphic which I have created for you. Let us consider a researcher

334
00:37:59,390 --> 00:38:06,350
who is working on viruses isolated from chicken and he cultures the virus in the laboratory.

335
00:38:06,350 --> 00:38:13,950
His assistant introduces new genes into the virus because their motive is purely scientific

336
00:38:13,950 --> 00:38:22,030
research. Now, what happens next is that these genes permit this virus from the chicken to be

337
00:38:22,030 --> 00:38:29,710
transmitted into animal host. This is purely research based study. So, they conduct their

338
00:38:29,710 --> 00:38:35,630
research, they check the transmission into simian, murine and bovine host. That is what they do.

339
00:38:35,630 --> 00:38:42,190
They conduct the test. Now, there is a breach of containment in this facility for instance.

340
00:38:42,190 --> 00:38:47,790
We assume that there is a breach of containment. This is the contained facility with all the

341
00:38:47,790 --> 00:38:52,270
precautions and there is a breach of containment. This may be either as a result of a breach of

342
00:38:52,270 --> 00:38:58,430
biosecurity or a biosafety breach. So, that is what happens. So, then you have your virus which

343
00:38:58,430 --> 00:39:06,210
can now infect humans and cause mortalities. Now, the virus has developed or been endowed

344
00:39:06,210 --> 00:39:11,490
with the ability to infect human host by an animal host and this is purely because a new

345
00:39:11,490 --> 00:39:15,970
gene was introduced. So, this is an example of a gain of function experiment.

346
00:39:19,180 --> 00:39:27,020
Okay, now in the case of genetic engineering, we focus on the risk assessment as the risk is very

347
00:39:27,020 --> 00:39:33,420
high and a genetic organism which contains specific traits such as increased violence

348
00:39:33,420 --> 00:39:38,620
must be treated with the highest level of caution. In bio risk management, we often refer

349
00:39:38,620 --> 00:39:43,580
to the iceberg hypothesis or basically the iceberg concept in which these are the risks

350
00:39:43,580 --> 00:39:47,420
which are visible above the water line in the case of iceberg and these are risks which are

351
00:39:47,420 --> 00:39:52,540
unknowns. So, in the case of GMOs or genetically modified organisms, there is a high number of

352
00:39:52,540 --> 00:39:57,580
unknown risks. So, these can lead to the inadvertent introduction of traits which may

353
00:39:57,580 --> 00:40:06,020
convert a benign microorganism into a potential pathogen. We now move on to the next part

354
00:40:06,020 --> 00:40:13,380
which is chemical, electrical and fire safety. In a conventional laboratory, these are hazards

355
00:40:13,380 --> 00:40:20,180
chemical, fire and electrical hazards are always present in any working environment. However, these

356
00:40:21,060 --> 00:40:28,260
hazards can lead to accidents and if you have a fire or chemicals fail and you invite your

357
00:40:28,260 --> 00:40:33,460
hazmat team over or you call a hazmat team to clean up, there is a risk of a breach of

358
00:40:33,460 --> 00:40:39,300
containment via the personnel. This is what needs to be taken into account when designing your

359
00:40:39,300 --> 00:40:46,100
standard operating procedures for contingencies. So, contingencies plan for these particular

360
00:40:46,100 --> 00:40:52,100
incidents such as chemical, fire and electrical safety. So, your hazmat teams can identify

361
00:40:52,100 --> 00:41:00,100
specific risks and you can train the first responders in these particular aspects of safety

362
00:41:00,100 --> 00:41:10,510
in the context of biological safety. The next part focuses on safety organization and training.

363
00:41:10,510 --> 00:41:14,910
So, in the safety organization and training which I have discussed in the laboratory

364
00:41:15,630 --> 00:41:21,070
virus management system lecture, the role of the biosafety officer is very important

365
00:41:21,070 --> 00:41:29,070
as the biosafety officer is the key element in the entire organization. The biosafety officer may

366
00:41:29,070 --> 00:41:37,950
work in coordination with a facility manager. Generally, a biosafety officer is a person or

367
00:41:37,950 --> 00:41:44,350
an individual with a background in the life sciences and the facility manager is an engineer.

368
00:41:44,350 --> 00:41:48,670
So, they both work in close coordination in order to maintain this facility.

369
00:41:49,310 --> 00:41:55,630
And we also have at the peak of this organization the IBC or the Institutional Biosafety Committee.

370
00:41:55,630 --> 00:42:04,510
Now, this IBC is basically a committee which focuses on the administration of the facility

371
00:42:04,510 --> 00:42:12,190
itself. So, you have your IBC at the peak here and then you have your biosafety officer here

372
00:42:12,750 --> 00:42:16,830
and then you have your laboratory workers, your technicians and your personnel.

373
00:42:16,830 --> 00:42:21,870
I have discussed this in much detail on in my other lecture which is available on YouTube.

374
00:42:21,870 --> 00:42:28,830
So, at the bottom of this chain of command is your laboratory workers, your laboratory cleaners,

375
00:42:28,830 --> 00:42:34,110
your technicians as well as your researchers and maybe other personnel who are associated

376
00:42:34,110 --> 00:42:38,830
with the maintenance of your facility. This is a very important structure and the roles

377
00:42:38,830 --> 00:42:45,070
and responsibilities of each of these components must be established in your laboratory biosafety

378
00:42:45,070 --> 00:42:51,710
manual. There is also the safety of bios support staff which must is must be highlighted as you

379
00:42:51,710 --> 00:42:57,310
may have to comply with local occupational safety and health guidelines and finally the training.

380
00:42:57,310 --> 00:43:03,630
So, training is not only conducted at the beginning of a particular experiment, it has

381
00:43:03,630 --> 00:43:11,710
to be an ongoing process because as I mentioned in my earlier lecture on the laboratory management

382
00:43:11,710 --> 00:43:24,220
system, training is a part of the continuous quality improvement of your personnel. The WHO

383
00:43:24,220 --> 00:43:33,100
laboratory biosafety manual gives us a large number of guidelines on the development of checklists.

384
00:43:33,100 --> 00:43:39,660
Now, checklists are basically associated with standard operating procedures. You may be aware

385
00:43:39,660 --> 00:43:47,340
of the checklists in a standard for instance in an aeroplane, the pilot undergoes a specific set

386
00:43:47,340 --> 00:43:52,780
of procedures and then he or she will complete a checklist. Now, this checklist will ensure that

387
00:43:52,780 --> 00:43:58,540
a procedure has been carried out and they improve what is known as situational awareness.

388
00:44:00,510 --> 00:44:07,710
Please be aware that working in a containment facility or a biological safety level 3 or 2

389
00:44:07,710 --> 00:44:14,110
laboratory involves a high degree of situational awareness and this situational awareness is

390
00:44:14,110 --> 00:44:20,990
determined by your focus and also your fatigue level and stress levels. For instance, if a

391
00:44:20,990 --> 00:44:30,670
laboratory procedure requires you to work for 3 hours, you must be aware of all the situations

392
00:44:30,670 --> 00:44:37,150
around you within those 3 hours and if the bio risk manager decides that 3 hours is too long

393
00:44:37,150 --> 00:44:44,030
for a particular procedure, he or she may implement a shift system in which the first laboratory

394
00:44:44,030 --> 00:44:50,030
worker works for 2 hours and the next laboratory worker takes off from that. Now, this management

395
00:44:50,030 --> 00:44:57,870
of time and work schedules is very important in a containment facility as fatigue and repetitive

396
00:44:57,870 --> 00:45:06,270
tasks can lead to a breach of containment. So, safety checklist will ensure that situational

397
00:45:06,270 --> 00:45:11,070
awareness is being maintained. For instance, if I work with a pathogen, the first procedure

398
00:45:11,070 --> 00:45:18,110
involves opening the tube. I check on the checklist. The next procedure involves the opening of the

399
00:45:18,110 --> 00:45:23,230
tube and transfer the media check. So, this checklist can be done by yourself or you can

400
00:45:23,230 --> 00:45:29,950
have a buddy observing you and checking the checklist. This checklist will improve the

401
00:45:30,030 --> 00:45:36,910
traceability of your process and will enable you to identify any procedures which may lead to

402
00:45:36,910 --> 00:45:42,990
potential accidents or incidents and you also have to comply with standard operating procedures.

403
00:45:42,990 --> 00:45:49,550
Please take note that all facilities may or will be audited periodically and the auditor

404
00:45:49,550 --> 00:45:56,510
will definitely require your checklists for examination. So, please maintain your checklists

405
00:45:56,510 --> 00:46:03,390
ensure that they are stored both in electronic format by scanning the documents as well as in the

406
00:46:03,390 --> 00:46:08,990
hard copy or printed format and they should be filed and these are controlled documents.

407
00:46:11,820 --> 00:46:17,260
Okay, to summarize the WHO laboratory biosafety manual provides a good foundation for the

408
00:46:17,260 --> 00:46:23,500
development of your own institutional biosafety manual. The WHO laboratory manual should be

